    "ls|LS16_S10_U_266": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.†*266  \nANSWERED ON 05.12.2016 \n \nPARYATAK MITRA \n \n†*266. DR. MANOJ RAJORIA:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has launched the Paryatak Mitra  \n(Tourist Facilitators) Scheme in the country and if so, the \ndetails of the scheme;  \n(b) the number of tourist facilitators in the country, State/UT -wise; \nand  \n(c)  whether any guidelines have been issued under the scheme \nand if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a)  to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n******** \n\n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO.†*266 ANSWERED ON 05.12.2016 REGARDING PARYATAK \nMITRA. \n \n(a) to (c): The Ministry of Tourism launched an initiative on the \noccasion of “National Youth Day” on 12th January, 2016. Titled \nParyatak Mitra Programme, it targets college -going students \nincluding those enrolled with the National Cadet Corps ( NCC) & \nNational Service Scheme ( NSS), in the age group of 18 -28 years. The \nprogramme attempts to inculcate appropriate tourism traits and \nknowledge among the trainees to enable them to act/work as \nTourist Facilitators (Paryatak Mitra). Each course is of 10 days \nduration. The programme is being implemented by the Indian \nInstitute of Tourism and Travel Management (IITTM), first at \ndestinations identified under the PRASAD Scheme of Ministry of \nTourism. Two batches of 119 students were trained in January, \n2016 at Vara nasi and Puri. The guidelines for the ‘Paryatak Mitra’ \ntraining programme are enclosed as Annexure. \n \n************ \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. †*266 ANSWERED ON 05.12.2016 \nREGARDING PARYATAK MITRA. \n \nA P ROGRAMME OF TRAINING TO BRING UP ‘PARYATAK MITRA’ \nGUIDELINES \n \ni. Objective \n \ni) To inculcate appropriate tourism traits and knowledge \namongst the  trainees to enable them to act /work as \nTourist Facilitators (Prayatak Mitra). \nii) To expect the young men and w omen so trained to act as \ncatalyst/resource persons in turn for similar effort.  \niii) To gradually work towards a tourism -sensitive citizenary \n \nii. Implementation \n \nThe following will be the authorities for implementation:  \n \na) Joint Secretary/Additional Dire ctor General, Government \nof India Ministry of Tourism (MoT) \n For the matters of policy, strategy and the size of the \nprogramme and the matters connected therewith.  \nb) HSRT Unit – Skill Development, MoT \nFor processing financial sanctions and issuing \nclarification on administrative aspect.  \nc) The programme Monitoring Unit – Skill Development, \nMoT. \nFor monitoring the programme \nd) The Director, IITTM for conduct of the programme and \ncertification \n \niii. Target Group & Eligibility \n \nCollege going student including  those enrolled with the NCC & \nNSS in the age group of 18 – 28 years. The age will be reckoned on \nthe last date prescribed for application for admission. The applicant \nwill also furnish a no -objection/sponsorship letter issued by th e \nPrincipal concerned as to his/her joining the programme. \n \n\niv. Training target \nInitially three - four batches of 30 each at the sites identified \nunder the Scheme National Mission on Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and \nSpiritual Augmentation Drive.  \n \nv. Publicity \n \nIITTM will provide adequ ate publicity to the programme \nthrough inserting advertisements in English and Hindi newspapers \nand also through its website. The IITTM will also contact various \ncolleges and Institutions and sensitize their Principals / Heads about \nthe programme and seek their support. The IITTM will be \nreimbursed the cost of advertisement in the newspapers.  \n \nvi. Selection \n \nThe selection will be done on a first come first serve basis.  \n \nvii. Course contents \n \nSome of the topics, to be covered during the programme, will \nbe \n \n1. Indian Society  and Culture \na) Important Monuments of India  \nb) Monuments of Delhi \nc) History of India \nd) Indian Art, Culture & Tradition  \ne) Dances, Music ,Sculptures of India  \nf) How to draw  up programmes/itineraries \ng) Dos and Don’ts at Monuments  \n \n2. An overview of Tourism Travel & Hospitality Industry  \na) Elementary knowledge of Travel & Tourism \nbusiness \nb) Frontier Formalities. Aviation Industry, Indian \nRailways, Surface \nc) Transportation, STDCs/ITDC \nd) Sports Tourism \n \n3. Managerial & Behavioural skills  \na) Handling Emergencies \n\nb) Inter- Personal behavior, Group behavior  \nc) Etiquettes and Manners \nd) Public Speaking/presentation \n \n4. Swachcha Bharat Abhiyaan \n5. Pride in Nation \n6. Gender Sensitization \n7. Responsible Tourism \n \nviii. Duration \n \nEach programme wi ll be of a duration of 10 working days ( 8 \nhours per day including lunch and refreshment breaks ).  \n \nix. Stipend \n \nEach trainee certified successful will be  paid on onetime \nstipend of Rs.1000/-. \n \nx. Certification \n \nThe trainees with minimum attendance of 90% w ill be required \nto appear in an end -of the - programme test which will comprise a \nwritten examination and presentation on a topic assigned. Each \nsuccessful trainee will be awarded a certificate by the IITTM. \nHowever,  Director, IITTM will have the discretio n, for reasons to be \nrecorded in writing, to relax the attendance norms by 10 % specific \nto eligibility for the end of the programme test.  \n \nxi. Pass-outs records \n \nAfter conclusion of training, the IITTM will ensure that the list \nof the pass -outs along with  their full contact details like address, \ntelephone number, mobile nu mber (excluding female trainees ) etc . \nand their employment and salary status, if any, are posted on its \nwebsite. \n \nxii. Employment Facilitation \n \ni) The IITTM will widely and regularly sens itize the \nstakeholders about this programme. It will also invite the \nindustry leaders/prospective employers in valedictory \nfunctions. \n \n\nii) The IITTM will forward the list of pass -outs with their \ncontact details to the State Tourism Departments \nconcerned, for being drawn up should there be a need for \ntourist facilitators. \n \nxiii. Funding of the programme \n \nThe Ministry of Tourism will extend assistance to the IITTM to \nenable it to meet the expenditure on the programme as per the \nfollowing norms: \n \ni) Training cost @ Rs. 500/ - per trainee per day Rs.5000/- (as \nper provision in para 20 (i) of the CBSP scheme)  \nii) Certification @ Rs. 500/ - per trainee Rs.500/- (as per \nprovision in para 20 (i) of the CBSP scheme)  \n \nSub total Rs.5500/- \n \niii) Administrative charges per  trainee Rs.275/- @ 5% of \nsubtotal \niv.) Stipend @ Rs 1000 per trainee Rs.1000/- (same as allowed \nfor a six week training course under HSRT)  \n \nTotal Rs.6775/- \n \nThe following will be admissible extra:  \n \ni) Cost of advertisement  on actuals subject to lowest/ DAVP \nrates. \nii) Training courses conducted at places other than NCR of \nDelhi, Gwalior, Bhubaneswar, Goa and Nellore, an \nadditional Rs. 100 per trainee per day.  \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_267": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 267\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.11.2016\nPROJECTS STALLED UNDER PMGSY IN MAHARASHTRA\n267. SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the work of construction of rural link roads in Buldhana district of\nMaharashtra under the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) is lying\npending or stalled;\n(b) if so, the reasons therefor and the reaction of the Government thereto; and\n(c) if not, the details of the roads constructed during the last year and the current year in\nthe said district under PMGSY?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a) to (c): ‘Rural roads’ is a State subject and the works under PMGSY are executed through\nState Governments. Central funds under PMGSY are released to the State Government.\nFurther release of these funds, to district and sub -district level is made by the State\nGovernment.\nThe details of funds released to the State Government of Maharashtra under PMGSY since\n2013-14 is as follows:-\n(Rs. in crore)\nFinancial Year Central share\nAllocation Release\n2014-15 221.00 212.53\n2015-16 553.30 553.30\n2016-17\n(upto Oct, 2016 )\n606.00 296.565\nThe State Government of Maharashtra has reported that 11 blocks of Buldhana district are in\nBuldhana Parliamentary Constituency and 2 Blocks are in Raver Parliamentary Constituency.\nThe upgradation work of 6 roads (46.25 KM) and 2 bridges , a total of 8 works were\nsanctioned under PMGSY-II during the year 2013-14 and 2014-15, in Buldhana district.\nThe State of Maharashtra has reported that out of 8 works, 5 works have been completed and\nremaining 3 works are in progress. The status of 8 road works is given at Annexure.\n*****\n\nANNEXURE\nAnnexure referred in reply of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Q. No.267\nSr.\nNo.\nName of Work Taluka Package\nNo.\nGOI Cost Date of\nWork Order\nExpenditure Work No of Village Benefited with\nPopulation\nProgress\nLength Cost\nVillage Population\n1 MDR-13 (Shendurjan) to\nTaluka Border (Khalegaon)\nRoad\nS.Raja MH 0740 425.56 16/08/2014 8.25 272.76 Jagdari\nBhandari\nKhandari\n1265 Work  Completed\n908\n607\n2 SH-228 to SH-274 (Karwand) Chikhali MH 0741 345.8 16/12/2014 7.45 150.57 Sawarkhed\nKavhala\n820 Work  Completed\n2555\n3 SH-24 Deulghat to Tandulwadi -\nSH-226 Road\nBuldana MH 0742 291.43 16/08/2014 4.75 153.31 Tandulwad 1559 Work in Progress\nIslampur 484\n4 MSH-13 (Parda) to SH -277\n(Taroda) Road\nMotala MH 0743 448.08 16/08/2014 8.55 125.07 Parda 816 Work in Progress\nShirwa 1014\nSaharstramuli 1426\n5 MSH-13 to Ghusar to Taluka\nBorder Pimpalkhuta Road Tq.\nMalkapur\nMalkapur MH 0744 400.07 29/08/2014 7.6 59.7 Pimpalkhuta\nBhadgani\n1134\n2868\nWork in Progress\n6 SH-271 (Jalgaon) –Wadshingi-\nSH-222 (Madakhed Bk) Road\nJalgaon\nJamod\nMH 0745 306.43 26/08/2014 9.65 84.87 Wadshingi 3424 Work  Completed\nMadakhed Bk 1619\n7 Cons of submerlible bridge SH -\n271 to Wadshingi – SH-222\n(Madakhed) Road Ch. 4/100\nJalgaon\nJamod\nMH 0746 83.39 21/08/2014 - 26.52 Wadshingi 3424 Work  Completed\nMadakhed Bk 1619\n8 Cons of submerlible bridge SH -\n271 to Wadshingi – SH-222\n(Madakhed) Road Ch. 9/100\nJalgaon\nJamod\nMH 0746 124.03 21/08/2014 - 66.74 Wadshingi 3424 Work  Completed\nMadakhed Bk 1619",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_268": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *268\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2016\n \n \nCREDIT LINKED CAPITAL SUBSIDY SCHEME\n \n \n \n*268. \n \nSHRI AJAY NISHAD:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to \nstate:\n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government is satisfied with the implementation of various schemes for \nthe development of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) and if so, the details \nthereof;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nwhether the Credit Linked Capital Subsidy Scheme for Technology is being \nimplemented by the Government for technology upgradation in MSMEs in the country and if \nso, the details thereof; and\n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nthe number of MSMEs covered under the scheme during the last thre\ne years and the \ncurrent year in the country, State/ UT\n-\nwise including Bihar?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI KALRAJ MISHRA)\n \n \n(a)to\n(c):\n \nA Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *268 DUE \nFOR REPLY ON 05\nTH\n \nDECEMBER, 2016 REGARDING CREDIT LINKED \nCAPITAL SUBSIDY SCHEME\n \n \n \n(a)\n:\n \nYes, Madam. \nThe Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSME) has \nbeen satisfactorily implementing various schemes and programmes for promotion and \ndevelopment of MSMEs. The major schemes include Prime Minister’s Employment \nGeneration Programme (PMEGP), Credi\nt Guarantee Scheme, Credit Linked Capital Subsidy \nScheme (CLCSS), National Manufacturing Competitiveness Programme, Micro and Small \nEnterprises \n-\n \nCluster Development Programme, Marketing Development Assistance and \nInternational Cooperation Scheme etc. Furt\nher, several pro\n-\nMSME steps to facilitate ease of \ndoing business have been initiated in recent years which include Udyog Aaadhar \nMemorandum, Finance Facilitation Centre, MSME Databank and Raw Material Credit to \nMicro and Small Enterprises. \n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nThe Minis\ntry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises is implementing Credit Linked \nCapital Subsidy Scheme (CLCSS) for technology upgradation of Micro and Small \nEnterprises. The Scheme launched in October, 2000 was revised in 2005. The revised scheme \nfacilitates tech\nnology upgradation of Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs) by providing 15% \ncapital subsidy (limited to maximum of Rs.15.00 lakhs) for purchase of plant & machinery. \n  \nMaximum limit of eligible loan for calculation of subsidy under the scheme is Rs.100.00 \nla\nkhs.\n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nThe State\n-\nwise details of number of MSEs benefitted under the Scheme during the \nlast three years and in the current year (upto 31.10.2016) in the country including Bihar is at \nAnnex\n-\n1.\n \n \n******\n \n \n \n\nAnnex\n-\n1\n \nAnnex\n-\nI referred to in reply to part (\nc\n) of the Lok Sabha \nstarred\n \nQuestion No. \n*268\n \nfor \nanswer on 05.12.2016\n \n \nState\n-\nwise details of MSEs benefitted under the CLCSS during the last three years and \nthe current year\n \n \nS.\n \nNo.\n \nState/UT\n \nNumber of MSEs benefitted\n \n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2015\n-\n16\n \n2016\n-\n17\n \n(\nupto 31.10.2016)\n \n1\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n203\n \n129\n \n38\n \n28\n \n2\n \nAndman & Nicobar\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n3\n \nArunanchal Pradesh\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n4\n \nAssam\n \n1\n \n0\n \n4\n \n0\n \n5\n \nBihar\n \n3\n \n0\n \n4\n \n1\n \n6\n \nChandigarh\n \n50\n \n22\n \n14\n \n4\n \n7\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n38\n \n59\n \n38\n \n7\n \n8\n \nDadra & Nagar \nHaveli\n \n4\n \n24\n \n8\n \n14\n \n9\n \nDaman & Diu\n \n20\n \n25\n \n22\n \n15\n \n10\n \nGoa\n \n3\n \n6\n \n2\n \n3\n \n11\n \nGujarat\n \n2154\n \n3060\n \n2046\n \n1617\n \n12\n \nHaryana\n \n325\n \n512\n \n314\n \n161\n \n13\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n3\n \n8\n \n2\n \n3\n \n14\n \nJ&K\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n15