    "ls|LS16_S10_U_279": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT OF INDUSTRIAL POLICY & PROMOTION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 279. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2016. \n \nBILATERAL TRADE AND INVESTMENT BETWEEN INDIA AND SRI LANKA \n \n*279.  SHRI ANANDRAO ADSUL: \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n    वाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ं ्री \n(a)  whether a meeting of the India-Sri Lanka Business Forum was held during the \nvisit of Sri Lankan Minister of  Development Strategies and International Trade \nto India; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the issues discussed therein; \n \n(c)  whether any progress has been made  on the proposed Economic and \nTechnology Co-operation Agreement (ETCA) between India and Sri Lanka; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof indicating  the number of round of negotiations for the  \nsaid ETCA; and \n \n(e)  the time by which ETCA is likely to be finalised? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ााज्‍ यं ्री‍स्‍‍ वतं ्‍्रभााा(स्रींतंरी‍िरंमलाा‍ीरीतंाांि(‍ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRIMATI NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n \n(a) to (e):   A  Statement  is  laid  on  the  Table  of  the  House. \n \n \n****** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT  REFERRED  TO  IN  REPLY  TO LOK  SABHA  STARRED  \nQUESTION NO.279 FOR  ANSWER ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2016. \n \n \n \n(a) & (b):   No, Madam.  Confederation of Indian Industry ( CII)  had  only \norganized  an  Industry  Interaction  with  H. E. Mr. Malik  \nSamarawickrama, Minister  of  Development  Strategies  and  \nInternational  Trade, Sri Lanka  on  5 th  July, 2016  in  New Delhi.  It  \nwas  not  a  Business  Forum  Meeting. \n \n(c) to (e):   India  and  Sri  Lanka  are  engaged  in  negotiating  the  text  of  the  \nproposed  Economic  and  Technology  Cooperation  Agreement \n(ETCA).  Two   rounds  of  negotiations  have  already  been  held  with \nthe objective of  expeditious  conclusion  of  the  Agreement. \n \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_280": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT OF INDUSTRIAL POLICY & PROMOTION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 280. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 5TH DECEMBER, 2016. \n \nFIRECRACKER COMPANIES \n \n*280.  SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n    वाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ं ्री \n(a)  whether the Government proposes  to make it compulsory for firecracker  \nmanufacturers to declare the chemicals used  in the firecrackers and also \nindicate warn ing of the adverse effect thereof on environment  and human \nhealth and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether the Government also  proposes to ban the use of chemicals in  \nfirecrackers which have an adverse effect  on environment and health and if \nso, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes  to take action against those indulging in  \nlarge scale illegal manufacture of firecrackers and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्‍ य‍वव ‍द्यग ‍ााज्‍ यं ्री‍स्‍‍ वतं ्‍्रभााा(स्रींतंरी‍िरंमलाा‍ीरीतंाांि(‍ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRIMATI NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n……..  \n\n      \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 280  FOR ANSWER ON 5TH DECEMBER, 2016.   \n \n \n(a): It has been made  mandatory for firecracker manufacturers to mention the \ncomposition of e xplosives and quantity of the same as per Rule 15 (4) of \nthe Explosives Rules, 2008.  However, it is not mandatory to m ention the \nconsequential effects of fireworks on the environment and human health \non the label of the packet or carton. \n   \n(b): Government of India vide Notification G.S.R. No.64 (E) dated 27th January, \n1992 prohibited the manufacture, possession and importation of any \nexplosive consisting of or containing sulphur or sulphurate in admixture \nwith chlorate of potassium or any other chlorate, provided that this \nprohibition shal l not extend to the manufacture or possession of such \nexplosives:  (a) in small quantities for scientific purposes; (b) for the \npurpose of manufacturing heads of matches; (c) for use in toy amorces \n(paper caps for toy pistols); or (d) in percussion caps fo r use in Railway \nFog Signals.  There is no proposal to ban the use of any other chemical in \nfirecrackers at present.  \n  \n(c): Illegal manufacturing of firecrackers is punishable with imprisonment for a \nterm which may extend to three years, or with fine whic h may extend to \nfive thousand rupees or with both, under Section 9B of the Explosives Act, \n1884. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_281": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 281\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.11.2016\nPROPOSALS UNDER RURAL SCHEMES\n281. SHRI LAXMI NARAYAN YADAV:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of proposals sent by the Government of Madhya Pradesh to the Union\nGovernment regarding Rural Development during the last three years;\n(b) the proposals cleared out of them and the details of pending proposals; and\n(c) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to clear the pending proposals?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a) to (c): The Minstry of Rural Development, inter alia, is implementing Mahatma\nGandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA), Deendayal Antyodaya\nYojana - National Rural Livelihood Mission (DAY-NRLM), Deen Dayal Upadhyay - Gramin\nKaushalya Yojana (DDU -GKY),  Indira Awaas Yojana (IAY) now restructured as  Pradhan\nMantri Awaas Yojana –Gramin (PMAY-G), Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY),\nShyama Prasad Mukherji Rurban Mission (SPMRM),  Start -up Village Entrepreneurship\nProgramme (SVEP) and National Social Assistance Programme (NSAP)  to bring about\noverall improvement in the quality of life of the people in rural areas through employment\ngeneration, strengthening of livelihood opportunities, promoting self employment, skilling of\nrural youths, provision of social assistance and other basic amenities.\nThe details o f proposals received from Government of Madhya Pradesh and their status are\ngiven at Annexure.\n******\n\nAnnexure\nAnnexure referred to in part (a) to (c) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No-281 due\nfor reply on 17.11.2016\nDAY-NRLM\nProposals under Livelihoods\nProposal Year Amount (Rs\nCrore)\nStatus\nSustainable livelihoods enhancement by NTFP\nunder World bank special fund for value chain\nintervention under Livelihoods\n2015-\n16\n3.5 Approved\nAnnual Action Plan under Mahila Kisan\nSashaktikaran Pariyojana (MKSP) for 2016-2017\nfor the State of Madhya Pradesh\n2016-\n17\n3.32 Under\nprocess\nProposal under DAY-NRLM for Financial Inclusion\nAmount in Rs. Lakhs\nTitle of\nProject\nProposed\nbudget\nStatus of\napproval\nby EC\nBudget\napproved\nby EC\nApproval for release Remarks\nCentral\nShare\nState\nShare\nTotal\nEnhancing\nFinancial\nInclusion\nthrough Bank\nCorrespondent\nAgents\n688 Approved 50 30 20 50 Central\nShare to\nbe\nreleased in\nsingle\ninstallment\nduring FY\n2016-17\nProposals sanctioned under SVEP\nState Block NRO*\nTotal Sanction (in\nLakhs) Central share (60%)\nMadhya Pradesh Karahal EDII** 510.76 306.46\nMadhya Pradesh Rajpur EDII 541.74 325.04\nMadhya Pradesh Samnapur EDII 466.68 280.01\n*National Resource Organization, **Entrepreneurship Development Institute of\nIndia\nProposals cleared under PMGSY\nYear Value (Rs.\nin crore)\nNo. of\nroads\nNo. of\nBridges\nLength in\nKm.\n2013-14 1,186 691 112 1,941\n2014-15 - - - -\n2015-16 2,403 1,681 83 4,391\n2016-17 1,842.91 875 173 2,861\nDDU-GKY:  The Department of Rural Development had received an Action Plan\nproposal for 2016 -19 from the Government of Madhya Pradesh to skill 91,947 rural\npoor youths. The same has been approved by the Department.",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_282": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 282\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.11.2016\nFUNDS UTILISATION BY UTTAR PRADESH UNDER RURAL SCHEMES\n282. SHRI BHAIRON PRASAD MISHRA:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the amount released till date to the villages of Uttar Pradesh out of the amount of at\nleast Rs.80 lakhs per village proposed to be allocated during the current Five Year\nPlan aimed at develop ment of villages as per a decision taken by the Government in\nthis regard;\n(b) whether the said amount has been transferred to the accounts of the Gram Pradhans;\nand\n(c) if so, the amount utilised so far out of the said funds?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a): There is no provision of transferring funds under rural development programmes\ndirectly to the villages.\n(b) and (c): Do not arise.\n*****",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_283": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 283 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.11.2016 \n \n \nStock of Coal \n \n \n283.   SHRI A.ARUNMOZHITHEVAN:    \n \n \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that Coal India Limited’s stock now stand at 55.3 \nmillion tonnes against 57.7 million tonnes that it opened the year with;       \n(b) if so, the details thereof;     \n(c) whether the CIL has been able to liquidate the stock through e-auctions;     \n(d) if so, the total quantity of coal liquidated through e -auction during 2015 -\n16; and       \n(e) the quantity of coal earmarked for e-auction for the year 2016-17?   \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE ( INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR COAL, POWER, NEW & \nRENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES   \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n(a)&(b): As on 01/11/2016, Coal India Limited’s stock stands at 39.24  MT \nagainst 57.64 MT  on 01/04/2016.  \n \n(c)&(d): CIL was able to liquidate the stock through e -auctions and other \ncommitments. The total quantity of coal dispatched through e -auctions during \n2015-16 was 68.45 MT.  \n \n(e): Under regular E -auction windows viz. Spot & Forward E -auctions, CIL \nintends to dispatch around 60 MT of coal, which is about 10% of its production \nin the fiscal year 2016-17. \n \n  \n<<>> \n  \n \n \n\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_284": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 284 \nANSWERED ON 17.11.2016 \n \nDAMS UNDER CONSTRUCTION \n \n284.       SHRI GOPAL SHETTY \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the number of dams under construction in the country as on date, State-wise;  \n(b) the year of start of their construction along with expenditure to be  incurred thereon including \nthe time by which the construction work of these dams is proposed to be completed;  \n(c) the names of these dams and the places where these are situated along with the extent of \namount spent so far by the Union as well as the State Governments; and  \n(d) the percentage of work executed with regard to their completion? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & \nGANGA REJUVENATION  \n \n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n(a) As per National Register of Large Dams (NRLD ) maintained by Central Water \nCommission, there are 313 large dams under construction in the country as per list enclosed in \nANNEXURE-I. \n \n(b) to (d) ‘Water’ being a State subject, planning, execution, operation and maintenance of water \nresources projects including dams and canals are undertaken by the concerned State \nGovernments as per their own resources and priorities. In order to supplement the efforts of State \nGovernments, Central Government provides technical and financial assistance to encourage \nsustainable development and efficient management of water resources through various schemes \nand programmes viz. Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) etc. \n \nThe information on the year of start of the construction, percentage of work completed, \ncompletion of construction,  and the amount spent on these dams is maintained by the respective \nState Governments/implementing Authorities, such as NHPC.   \n \n***** \n\nANNEXURE-I \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) in respect of LokSabhaUnstarred Question \nNo.284 on Dams under Construction for answer on 17.11.2016. \n \n \nNumber of under construction large dams state wise as per National Register of Large \nDams (NRLD)  \nSl.No. Name of state No. of Dams as per NRLD \n1 Andhra Pradesh 25 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 3 \n3 Assam 1 \n4 Bihar 2 \n5 Chhattisgarh 10 \n6 Gujarat 13 \n7 Himachal Pradesh 1 \n8 Jammu & Kashmir 3 \n9 Jharkhand 29 \n10 Karnataka 1 \n11 Kerala 1 \n12 Madhya Pradesh 8 \n13 Maharashtra 152 \n14 Manipur 1 \n15 Mizoram 1 \n16 Odisha 5 \n17 Punjab 2 \n18 Rajasthan 10 \n19 Telangana 20 \n20 Uttara Pradesh 15 \n21 Uttarakhand 9 \n22 West Bengal 1 \n TOTAL 313 \n \n \n \n  \n\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_285": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 285 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06th DECEMBER, 2016 \n \nPUBLIC DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM \n \n*285.  SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL: \nSHRI OM BIRLA: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION  miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware  of distribution of poor quality \nfoodgrains under the Public Distribution System  (PDS) and if so, the \ndetails thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(b)  whether the Government has  prescribed any standards quality of  \nfoodgrains to be distributed under PDS and  if so, the details thereof and \nthe steps taken by the Government in this regard; \n \n(c)  the nature of various irregularities/corruption/malpractices noticed \nin the implementation of PDS and the corrective  steps taken/proposed \nto plug the same; and \n \n(d)  whether the Government proposes  to involve private companies in \nthe supply chain management in PDS and if so, the details thereof ? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*** \n\nAnnex-III \n \nANNEX REFERRED TO IN REPLY  TO PART (a)& (b) OF STARRED QUESTION NO 285 TO \nBE ANSWERED ON 06TH DECEMBER, 2016 IN LOK SABHA. \n \nSTATE-WISE DETAILS OF THE COMPLAINTS REGARDING DISTRIBUTION OF POOR \nQUALITY FOODGRAINS UNDER THE PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM (PDS) AN D \nACTION TAKEN BY  GOVERNMENT DURING THE YEAR 2015-16  \n \nState Complaint Action Taken by the Government \nNCT, Delhi \n \n \n \n \n \nMaharashtra \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nJammu & \nKas hmir \n \n \n \n \nKerala \nA complaint was received from \nIndraprastha Welfare Association, \nNew Delhi regarding S upply of \npoor quality of foodgrain in Moti \nNagar, Area of Delhi  \n \nA complaint received from \nSecretary, Food Civil Supplies and \nConsum er Pr otection Depar tm ent, \nGovt. of Maharashtra -  regarding \nsupply of luster loss  wheat from \nFCI base depot, Jalgoan and \nMSWC, Nanded. \n \n \nA complaint was received from \nDirector of Consumer affair & P.D. \nDeptt, Kashmir -\nregarding Supply \nof below standard quality wheat \nunder Wheat Relaxation Scheme. \n \nA complaint was received from \nKerala Ra tion Wholesale \nDistributor Federation, Kottayam \nin FCI regarding five bags of \nda mage d rice  f ro m FSD  \nValianthura to PDS. \nThe complaint was got investigated \nby deputing an officer of the \nMinistry. The complaint was not \nfound to be true.  \n \n \nInstructions we re issued to all State \nGovernments to accept the luster loss \nwheat procured during RMS 2015- 16 \nunder the relaxed specifications as \nluster lost wheat is as nutritious as \nsound wheat only s hining is less and \nthe quality of wheat is intact and \ngood.   \n \n \n-do- \n \n \n \n \nThe complaint was got investigated \nby Area Manager \nThiruvanathapuram and found that \nstock were lifted by State \nGovernm ent nominee after  pr oper \nacknowledgement about their \nsatisfaction on quality.  \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_286": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 286 \nANSWERED ON 17TH NOVEMBER, 2016 \n  \nROLE OF CONSTRUCTION COMPANIES FOR COMPLETION OF NH PROJECTS  \n \n286. SHRI R. DHRUVA NARAYANA: \nSHRI BHEEMRAO B. PATIL: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the work of national  highways projects have been judged as  completed even if these \nare not completed; if so, the reasons therefor; \n(b)  whether the CAG have said in its  report that the construction companies  have been paid crores \nof rupees as bonus for these projects, if so, the reasons therefor; \n(c) the amount paid to such companies  as bonus instead levying of fine and the  names of such \ncompanies; and \n(d) the highways for which this amount has been paid? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n(a) The Independent Engineer issues Provisional Completion Certificate/ Completion Certificates of \nthe projects after ensuring t he project highway  that can be legally, safely and reliably placed in \nCommercial Operation as per the provisions of the concession/ contract agreements.  \n \n(b) to (d ) The CAG in  its Report No. 15 of 2016 (Volume II) ( Para No. 2.1 & 2.2)   have pointed \nout  payment of  Bonus to following two concessionaires for early completion of the project:- \n \n(i)   M/s Mokama - Munger Highway Limited for construction of two -lane road on the \nMokama - Munger Section of NH -80 in Bihar – Bonus of Rs. 21.83 crore was paid by NHAI \nfor early completion of the project as per the provisions of the concession agreement. \n \n(ii)  M/s Hazaribagh Ranchi Expressway Limited, Ranchi (the Concessionaire for construction, \nof four laning Hazaribagh – Ranchi Section of NH – 33 in the State of Jharkhand. Bonus of \nRs. 47.05 crore was paid by NHAI for early completion of the project as per the provisions of \nthe concession agreement. \n  \nThe bonus was released by National Highway Authority of India ( NHAI) as per the provisions of \nconcession agreement and on the recommendations of Independent Engineer.  Action Taken Note of \nC&AG Paras have been furnished to C&AG justifying release of bonus. \n \n*****   ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_287": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 287 \nANSWERED ON 17TH    NOVEMBER 2016 \n \nWHO REPORT AND ROAD SAFETY IN INDIA \n \n287.   SHRI C.S. PUTTA RAJU: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the World Health Organization (WHO) has released its global report on road safety \nstating how road crashes were claiming majority of youth across the globe and in India as well; \n \n(b) whether India has poor distinction of reporting maximum fatalities on roads due to several \nreasons, if so, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(c) whether the Government has issued a fresh circular asking all agencies managing National \nHighways to comply with road  safety norms during design, construction and operation of \nstretches; if so, the details thereof ? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n (SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a)   As per global status report on road safety  2015, released by World Health Organization \n(WHO),  road traffic crashes are a leading cause of death among young people, and the main cause of \ndeath among those aged 15 –29 years across the globe.  The highest rate  of road traffic crashes is in the \nAfrican Region i.e. number of traffic crashes per 100000 population.  \n(b)    As per global status report on road safety 2015, the total fatalities reported in 2013 in \nIndia is 1,37,572 and the estimated fatality is 2,07,551.  As per the report “Road Accident  in India -\n2015”, the major causes of road crash fatalities are fault of drivers (72.6% ), fault of cyclist (1%), fault of \ndrivers of other vehicles (4.8%), fault of pedestrians (1.8%),  defect in condition of Motor vehicle (2.8%), \ndefect in road condition (1.9%), other causes / causes not known (9.2%), etc.   \n (c)  Road safety features and consideration are important part of Indian Roads Congress (IRC) \ncodes and Ministry of Road Transport & Highways guidelines which are followed in design, \nconstruction & operation of National Highways.   \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_288": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.288 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17TH NOVEMBER, 2016 \nDEVELOPMENT OF PORTS \n288.  SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN:  \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state: \nपोतप�रवहनमंत्  \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to develop a number of new and small ports \nfor commercial shipping transportation;  \n(b) if so, the details and the present status thereof;  \n(c)  the financial implication involved thereon, port-wise; and  \n(d)  the expected generation of income, foreign exchange and employment after \noperationalisation of these ports? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a)  Yes, Madam. Based on the traffic and cargo scenario of the 12 Major Ports, a \nMaster Plan has been prepared for expansion of port capacity, which includes a \nnumber of new ports.  \n \n(b)to(d):The details and the present status are enclosed at Annexure.   \n  \n\nANNEXURE   \nDetail of the Major Ports proposed to be developed, their Present Status, Financial Implication and \nOutcome of the Project \nMajor Port \nproposed to be \ndeveloped \nProposing Port Present Status Financial Implication Outcome of the \nproject \nSagar Island \n(West Bengal) \nKolkata Port PIB has appraised the \nproject subject to the \ncondition that state \nGovernment shall ensure \npossession of the land is \navailable to NHAI or any \nother Agency providing \nroad connectivity and to \nrailways/agency providing \nrail c onnectivity. State \nGovernment should give \nan undertaking to provide \n1st right of refusal to \nBhorSagar Port Ltd. in \ncase it decided to set up / \npermit setting up of any \nnew port in the state.  \nTotal Capital Cost of \nPhase – I  -  1464 \nCrore {Rs. 949 Core( \nto be borne by \nconcessionaire + Rs. \n515 (to be borne by \nSPV)} \n \n \n \n \n \nProjected Traffic is \nto be around 3.5 \nMTPA in 2020 \nincreasing to around \n27 MTPA in 2035.  \nThe Port will reduce \nthe constraints faced \nby Kolkata Port like \nlong river \nnavigation, \navailable draft  \nnavigation due to \npersistent siltation \nand high dredging \ncost.   \n \nEnayam near \nColachel, \n(Tamilnadu) \nV. O. Chidambar -\nanar Port \nDetailed Project Report \n(DPR) is under \npreparation. The first \ncontainer berth in Phase -I \nis expected to be \noperational by Dec. 2020.  \nThe Port is proposed \nto be developed \nunder the Landlord \nmodel.  As per \nTechno-Economic \nFeasibility Report, \ntotal project cost is \nRs. 27,570 Crores \nand t he estimated \ncost of Phase - 1 \ndevelopment is \nRs.6575Cr. \nExpected income \ngeneration of Rs . \n1149 Cr per annum \nby the year 2020 as \nper TEFR. EXIM \ntrade will increase \nand this will lead to \nincrease \nemployment and \nother benefits to the \nnational economy.  \nParadip Outer \nHarbour \n(Odisha) \nParadip Port Trust Techno Economic \nFeasibility Report (TEFR) \nhas been prepared. \nDetailed Project Report \n(DPR) will be completed \nby 30.05.2017.   \nProject cost for \ninitial phase is Rs. \n4179 Crore and for \nfinal phase is Rs. \n8767 Crore.  \nCapacity of the Port \nto be augmented \nfrom 140 MTPA to \n250 MTPA by \n2020. \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_289": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 289 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.11.2016 \n \n“Flexibility in use of Domestic Coal” \n \n289. SHRIMATI VANAROJA R: \n \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether the Government has approved a proposal to create flexibility in the use \nof domestic coal; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the said move is expected to reduce the cost of power generation ; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR COAL, POWER, NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nAND MINES \n \n( SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a)&(b):Yes, Madam.The Cabinet on 4th May, 2016 approved the proposal of the \nMinistry of Power for flexibility in utilization of domestic coal for reducing the cost of \npower generation. As per the policy, the Annual Contracted Quantity (ACQ) of the each \nindividual coal linkages (as per respective Fuel Supply Agreements) are to be \naggregated as consolidated ACQ for each State or the company owning the Central \nGenerating Stations (CGS). For such conso lidated ACQ, a Supplementary agreement \nshall be signed for each State/Company owning the CGS. There shall be a flexibility, in \nthe revised arrangement , for use of such coal amongst the generating stations of the \nstate owned utilities, plants of other state  power utilities, company owing the Central \nGenerating Stations and Independent Power Producers, amongst each others as per \nthe methodology issued by CEA. A methodology for implementing the above proposal \nhas been prepared by a committee consisting of the members from MoP, MoC, Ministry \nof Railways, CERC, NTPC, CIL, POSOCO in consultation with the stakeholders. This \nmethodology has been issued on 08 June, 2016. \n(c)& (d): The mechanism envisages to reduce the cost of power generation by using \ncoal in an optim al manner depending upon the efficiency of the generating stations. \nCentral/State generating companies may utilize coal in its own generating stations by \nconsidering various factors such as operational efficiency of the generating stations, \ntransportation logistics/ feasibility depending upon location of generating stations, fixed \nvariable charges including transportation cost, relative merit order dispatch of power etc. ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_290": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 290\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17.11.2016\nAPPRAISAL OF SAGY\n290. SHRI KIRTI AZAD:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether even after the completion of the first phase of Saansad Adarsh Gram Yojana\n(SAGY) the implementation of this scheme is not as per the objectives set for the\nscheme;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the reasons therefor;\n(c) whether complaints have been received from public representatives in this regard;\n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the action taken thereon; and\n(e) the steps being taken by the Government for proper financing and implementation of\nthe said scheme?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a) to (e) : SAGY is primarily about unleashing the power of people who are expected to\ninculcate pride in village, encourage societal change/Behavioural change, take collective\nresponsibility and initiate People projects. The development of Gram Panchayats identified\nunder SAGY is intended to take place through the convergence    and   implementation of\nexisting Government Schemes and Programmes without allocating additional funds.\nSo far 704 Gram Panchayats have been identified under phase - I of the SAGY\nprogram . Out of which  658 Gram Panchayats  have prepared their  Village Development\nPlans (VDP) across the States/UTs cumulatively comprising more t han 40,000 projects, out\nof which 33% of works are completed and 19% of works are in -progress.The progress of\nVillage Development Plan shows the impressive result in terms of convergence, resource\nenveloping and community participation and the interest of the Members of Parliament.\nThe Ministry has been receiving  queries/suggestions  from various stakeholders\nregarding the convergence of various existing schemes mainly relating to the basic services,\nviz., health, water, electricity, roads, housing and e ducation and the same is being acted\nupon/resolved from time to time.  Some of the steps taken in this regard are as under:-\ni. 21 Schemes have been amended by various Ministries /Departments of\nGovernment of India to give priority to SAGY Gram Panchayat projects.\nii. The Ministry of Rural Development has brought out ‘SAMANVAY’ -\nCompilation of 223 Central Sector/ Centrally Sponsored and 1806 State\nSchemes for convergence under SAGY. This document serves as a ready\nreckoner especially for the Members of Parli ament, District and Village level\nofficials about the different schemes for multitude of purposes at the GP level.\niii. A National Workshop was organised on 23rd -24th September, 2015 at Bhopal\nby MoRD, where Members of Parliament, State Governments, District\nCollectors, Gram Pradhans from across all States were invited. The selected\nGood Practices by National Level Committee of the Ministry of Rural\n\nDevelopment were presented through two days’ detailed exhibition so that\nsimilar good practices could be adopted in SAGY Gram Panchayats.\niv. With the intend to tap the resources and the strengths of the Private, Voluntary\nand Cooperative sectors (PVC Sectors), the Ministry has circulated suggestive\ntemplate to state governments for preparing proposals for tapping support\nfrom Private, Voluntary and Cooperative (PVC) sectors and received projects\nproposed by District Administration which detail availability / unavailability\nof fund, technical resources, rationale behind PVC Sector support etc. The\nproposals after examinati on have been displayed on the SAGY website for\nwider circulation\nv. The Ministry has prepared a  document  named ‘SAHYOG’ which is an\nindicative guidance document with the essential information  on the existing\nsocial security schemes collated from res pective ministries. It will enrich the\nknowledge of villagers and village level functionaries to achieve 100%\nenrollment into  the social/financial Security Schemes in SAGY GPs. It is\nbeing  circulated amongst the various stakeholders of SAGY .\n*****",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_291": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 291 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 17TH NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \nEXCAVATION OF MINERALS  \n \n†291. SHRI SADASHIV LOKHANDE: \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state:  \n(a) the details of minerals found in the country during the last three years, State/ \nlocation/mineral-wise;  \n(b) the steps taken by the Government for proper excavation of these minerals; and  \n(c) the names of agencies through which these works are likely to be undertaken? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER, COAL, \nNEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n  \n(a): Geological Survey of India (GSI), an attached office of Ministry of Mines , carries  out \nmineral exploration (survey) work in different States/Union Territories of the country as per its \nannual Field Season Programmes. The details of mineral s found/survey conducted by GSI in the \ncountry during the last three years are annexed.  \n \n(b): Excavation of minerals is do ne through grant of mining leases on the establishment of \nevidence to prove the existence of mineral contents in the explored areas. In areas where the \nevidence of mineral contents is not as per the level prescribed for grant of a mining lease, the state \ngovernments grant a prospecting licence -cum-mining lease which entails conducting prospecting \noperations to establish the evidence of mineral contents prescribed for grant of a mining lease \nbefore grant of a mining lease.  \n \n(c): The following Government companies have been notified under the second proviso to section \n4(1) of the MMDR Act, 1957 to conduct prospecting operations without a prospecting licence. \nThe Government companies are required to hand over the data generated by it, in respect of the \nprospecting operations undertaken by it, to the State Government which would enable the State \nGovernments to grant mining leases in areas where the ev