    "ls|LS16_S10_U_476": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 476\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH NOVEMBER, 2016\nUSE OF ALUMINIUM FOIL IN FOOD PACKAGING\n476. SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware that aluminium foil is used extensively for protection,\npacking and storage of food and beverages etc., if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the Government has laid down any guidelines for the manufacturing of aluminium\nfoils for wrapping food and beverage items, if so, the details thereof;\n(c) the number of aluminium foil samples collected by the Government for checking and found\nnot fit for use along with the action taken against the manufacturers during the last three years\nand the current year; and\n(d) the steps taken by the Government to educate consumers about the ill effects of using poor\nquality aluminium foils?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) & (b): Aluminium foil is commonly used for packing and storage of food. There are no\nspecifications for manufacture of Aluminium Foil as per the Food Safety and Standards Act, and\nRegulations thereunder. However, Bureau of Indian Standards BIS IS 15392 (2003) provides for\nspecifications for Aluminium and Aluminium Alloy foil for food packaging which are not under\nmandatory certification.\n(c): Enforcement of  the provisions of Food Safety and Standards Act, Rules and Regulations\nthereunder, is primarily the responsibility of the  State/UT Governments. Since there are no\nmandatory standards for Aluminium Foil,  the Food Safety and Standards Authority of India\n(FSSAI) has  no information/data about  collection of samples of Aluminium Foil by the\nState/UT Authorities .\n(d): FSSAI is committed to creating awareness among the general public about safety of food\nproducts. For this purpose, it makes use of numerous platforms like it ’s website, Twitter,\nFacebook page, Youtube, etc. FSSAI has also joined awareness campaigns under ”Jago Grahak\nJago” with the Department of Consumer Affairs in respect of food items. However, no separate\ncampaign has been launched concerning aluminium foils.\n******",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_477": " \n \n(a) whether the Wholesale Price Index (WPI) has increased whereas the retail and food inflation have \nshown a drop during the last three months in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n(c) the steps taken/being taken by t he Government to control WPI and to sustain the retail and food \ninflation to relieve the consumers from rising costs of fruits, vegetables and other essential \ncommodities? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL) \n \n(a) & (b) The Wholesale Price Index (WPI)  and inflation based on  WPI, Consumer Price Index (CPI) \nand Consumer Food Price Index (CFPI) during the last three months in the country is given in Table 1. \n \n \nTable 1: WPI and Inflation based on  \nWPI and CPI (Combined) \n  ------------------------------------------------------------------ \n                        Aug-16       Sep-16     Oct-16 \n  ------------------------------------------------------------------ \n  WPI                       183.3          182.8          182.9 \n \n  WPI inflation (%)        3.9             3.6             3.4 \n \n  CPI inflation (%)         5.0             4.4             4.2 \n \n  CFPI inflation (%)         5.9             4.0             3.3 \n \n  WPI Vegetables  \n  inflation (%)                0.2           -10.9          -10.0 \n \n  CPI Vegetables  \n  inflation (%)               1.0             -7.1             -5.7 \n--------------------------------------------------------------------- \nNote: Figures for WPI for last two months and CPI, CFPI for last one month is provisional.   \nSource: Department of Industrial Policy & Promotion, Central Statistics Office.  \n \nThe wholesale price index has marginally increased in October 2016 on account of rise in price of fuel \n& power and manufactured products. CPI headline inflation and food inflation based on CFPI has \ndeclined in the last three months mainly on account of decline in price of vegetables and pulses. \n \n(c) The Government has taken a number of measures to control inflation. The steps taken, inter alia, \ninclude, (i) increased allocation of Rs. 900 crore for Price Stabilization Fund in the budget 2016 -17 to \ncheck volatility of prices of essential commodities, in particular of pulses; (ii) created buffer stock of \npulses through domestic procurement and imports; (iii) announced higher Minimum Support Prices so \nas to incentivize production; (iv) issued advisory to States/UTs to take strict action against hoarding \nand black marketing under the Essential Commodities Act 1955 and the Prevention of Black-marketing \nand Maintenance of Supplies of Essential Commodities Act, 1980; (v) imposed 20 per cent duty on \nexport of sugar; (vi) imposed minimum export price (MEP) of US$ 360 per tonne on potato; and (vii) \nreduced import duty on potatoes, wheat and palm oil. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_478": "भारत सरकार\n \n\u0000व\u0000 मं\u0000ालय\n \nराज\u0000व \u0000वभाग\n \nलोक सभा\n \nअतारां\u0000कत \u0000\u0000न सं\n. \n478\n \n(\nिजसका उ\u0000र शु\u0000वार\n,\n \n18 \nनव\u0000बर\n,\n \n2016\n/\n27 \nका\u0000त\u0000क\n,\n \n1938 \n(\nशक\n) \nको \u0000दया जाना है\n)\n \n \nअसूचीब\u0000ध शेयर\u0000 से आय\n \n478\n. \nडॉ\n॰ क े॰ गोपालः\n \n \n\u0000या \u0000व\u0000 मं\u0000ी यह बताने क\u0000 कृपा कर\u0000गे \u0000कः\n \n(\nक\n) \n \n\u0000या \nसरकार का असूचीब\u0000ध शेयर\u0000 क\u0000\n \n\u0000ब\u0000\u0000 से \u0000ा\u0000त आय को पूंजीगत लाभ बनाने और\n \nइस पर \u0000यावसा\u0000यक आय क\u0000 तुलना म\u0000 \u0000न\u0000न दर\n \nपर कर लगाने का \u0000वचार है\n; \nऔर\n \n(\nख\n)\n \nय\u0000द हां\n, \nतो त\u0000संबंधी \u0000यौरा \u0000या है और\n \nइसक े \u0000या कारण ह\u0000\n?\n \n \n \nउ\u0000र\n \n \n\u0000व\u0000 मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी \n(\n\u0000ी सं\nतोष कुमार गंगवार\n)\n \n \n \n(\nक\n) \n \nजी नह\u0000ं\n, \nअसूचीब\u0000ध शेयर\u0000 \nक\u0000 \u0000ब\u0000\u0000 \nसे होने वाल\u0000 आय के बारे म\u0000 वत\u0000मान कर \u0000यव\u0000था \nम\u0000 कोई प\u0000रवत\u0000न करने का सरकार का \u0000वचार नह\u0000ं है।\n \n(\nख\n) \n \nयह \u0000\u0000न नह\u0000ं उठता है।\n \n \n \n \n*******\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_479": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 479\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH NOVEMBER, 2016\nNRHM\n479. SHRI RAJU SHETTY:\nSHRIMATI NEELAM SONKER:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware that there is acute shortage of female doctors, specialists,\nparamedics and other doctors under the National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) in St ate\nhospitals, Primary Health Centres (PHCs)/ Community Health Centres (CHCs);\n(b) if so, the State/UT-wise details thereof along with the reasons therefor;\n(c) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to meet the said shortage;\n(d) the number of sanctioned posts of doctors/specialists female doctors lying vacant in rural\nhealth centres, PHCs and CHCs of the country under NRHM and the time by which the said\nposts are likely to be filled; and\n(e) whether the cases of doctors remaining absent from the above centres have come to the notice\nof the Government and if so, the details thereof and the action being taken by the Government in\nthis regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(c) & (d) Public health being a State subject, the primary responsibility to ensure availability\nof health human resources in public health facilities lies with the State Governments.\nHowever, under the National Health Mission (NHM), financial and technical support is\n(a) & (b) As per Rural Health Statistics (RHS), there is a shortage of doctors (including\nfemale doctors) specialists, paramedics and other doctors in Primary Health Centres (PHCs)/\nCommunity Health Centres (CHCs). State/UT-wise details as per RHS 2015 is annexed.\nVarious reasons attributed for shortage of doctors, specialists, paramedics and other doctors\nin public health facilities, particularly in rural areas include overall shortage of doctors/\nspecialists/ paramedics in the country, feeling of professional isol ation among doctors &\nspecialists, and unwillingness on their part to work in rural areas.\n\nprovided to States/UTs to strengthen their healthcare systems including support for\nengagement of health human resources on contractual basis, based on the requirements\nposed by the States/UTs in their Programme Implementation Plans (PIPs). Support is also\nprovided to States/UTs by giving hard area allowance to health human resources for serving\nin rural and remote areas and for their residential quarter s so that they find it attractive to\nserve in public health facilities in such areas. As the posts required for health facilities are\nfilled up by respective State/UT Governments, they are impressed upon from time to time to\nfill up the vacant posts.\nFurther, in order to encourage doctors to work in remote and difficult areas, the Medical\nCouncil of India, with the previous approval of Central Government, has amended the Post\nGraduate Medical Education Regulations, 2000 to provide:\n(i) 50% reservation in Post Graduate Diploma Courses for Medical Officers in the\nGovernment service, who have served for at least three years in remote and difficult\nareas; and\n(ii) Incentive at the rate of 10% the marks obtained for each year in service in remote or\ndifficult areas as upto the maximum of 30% of the marks obtained in the entrance test\nfor admissions in Post Graduate Medical Courses.\n(iii) Details of sanctioned and vacant posts of doctors/specialis ts/paramedics are at\nAnnexure\n(e) Public health being a State subject, no such data is maintained at the central level.\n******\n\nAnnexure-I\nTable 21.\nDOCTORS+ AT PRIMARY HEALTH CENTRES\nS. No. State/UT\n(As on 31st March, 2015)\nRequired1 Sanctioned In\nPosition Vacant Shortfall\n[R] [S] [P] [S-P] [R-P]\n1 Andhra Pradesh 1069 2270 1412 858 *\n2 Arunachal Pradesh 117 NA 102 NA 15\n3 Assam 1014 NA 1355 NA *\n4 Bihar### 1883 2078 2521 