    "ls|LS16_S10_U_493": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 493\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH NOVEMBER, 2016\nUNFAIR PRACTICES TO PROMOTE MEDICINES\n493. SHRI BADRUDDIN AJMAL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Indian Drugs Manufactures Association (IDMA) has laid down certain norms\nwith regard to marketing practices for pharmaceutical companies in the country, if so, the details\nthereof;\n(b) whether the pharmaceutical companies are indulging in unfair practices to promote their\nmedicines by sponsoring trips and offering gifts to doctors;\n(c) if so, the steps taken by the Government to stop such unfair practices in the country;\n(d) whether the Government has proposed any new code of conduct for the marketing of drugs;\nand\n(e) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a): The Indian Drugs Manufacturers Association (IDMA) is an association of private\npharmaceutical manufacturers and the Government is not involved in the conduct of its affairs.\n(b): The Department of Pharmaceuticals has informed that there were some reports of\nPharmaceutical Companies indulging in unfair practices to promote their medicines.\n(c) to (e): The Department of Pharmaceuticals announced a Uniform Code of\nPharmaceutical Marketing Practices (UCPMP) w.e.f. 1st January, 2015 to curb unfair practices.",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_494": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE  \nLOK  SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.494 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 18TH NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \n \nINDIAN AIR FORCE TRAINING COMMAND \n \n494. KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE: \n  \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE   j{kk ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Union Government has received any proposal from the State \nGovernment of Karnataka to alter its property to accommodate a steel bridge in Indian \nAir Force Training Command Headquarters in Bengaluru ; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the action taken by the Government thereon? \n \n       A    N    S    W    E    R \n \n        MINISTER OF STATE             (DR. SUBHASH BHAMRE) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF DEFENCE \n      र\f ा रा\f य मं\fी          (डा. सुभाष भामरे) \n \n(a) No, Madam. \n(b) & (c): Do not arise. \n \n******** ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_495": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 495\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH NOVEMBER, 2016\nEXIT EXAM FOR MBBS\n495. SHRI Y.V. SUBBA REDDY:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes Exit Exam for MBBS pass out students, if so, the details\nthereof;\n(b) the extent to which it is likely to help in addressing the concerns over the standard of medical\ncolleges and quality of doctors;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to have separate exam for foreign medical graduates and\nIndian medical graduates;\n(d) if so, the reasons therefor; and\n(e) the extent to which it is likely to help in ending Inspector Raj in the medical education sector?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) to (e): In the draft National Medical Commission Bill considered by NITI Aayog, an\nExit Exam has been provided for at the under graduate level.  It is expected to help as a method\nfor quality certification of graduating doctors.  No separate exam for foreign graduate is provided\nfor in the draft bill. Exit exam would be an outcome based quality control mechanism instead of\nfocusing on verification of infrastructure.",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_496": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 496 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.11.2016 \n \nSTUDY BY NCPCR \n \n496. SHRI SHIVKUMAR UDASI: \n \n     Will the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n \na. whether the Government has taken note that children living in the areas of unrest in the \ncountry face  multiple problems, including health related problems; \nb. if so, whether National Commission for Protection of Child Rights (NCPCR) has \ninvestigated and studied the issues of these children; and \nc. if so, the details thereof along with the action taken to provide these children with basic \ncare and health facilities? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n              (SHRIMATI MANEKA SANJAY GANDHI) \n \n(a) : Yes , Madam. \n(b) & (c ): The National Commission for Protection of Child Rights (NCPCR) has not conducted \nany study on the issue of children living in the areas of unrest. However, a policy Document on \n“Protection of Children’s Rights in Areas of Civil  Unrest ” laid down guidelines and \nrecommendations addressed to different Ministries and State Governments which was published \nby NCPCR in 2010. Further,  the  Ministry of Health and Family Welfare is implementing \nvarious programs/ interventions under its N ational Health Mission(NHM) all across the country \nfor providing care and health facilities to children, including those living in the areas of unrest \nviz : Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakaram (JSSK), Universal Immunization Programme (UIP), \nRashtriya Bal Swa sthya Karyakram (RBSK), Iron and folic acid (IFA) & Vitamin -A \nSupplementation to vulnerable age group, Nutrition Rehabilitation Centres etc. \n******* \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_497": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Financial Services \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 497 \nTo be answered on the November 18, 2015/Kartika 27, 1938 (SAKA) \nSubject:   RRBs \nQuestion \n \n†497: SHRI K. PARASURAMAN:  \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a)   whether the Regional Rural Banks (RRBs) have adequate infrastructure as of other \ncommercial banks; \n \n(b)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c)  whether the Government prop oses to create a national -level apex body for RRBs in \nassociation with National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) so as to \nexpand its credit facilities in rural areas; \n \n(d)   if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e)   the steps taken by the Government to strengthen RRBs? \n \nAnswer \n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of Finance \n(SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n(a) & (b):   By March 2016, 56 RRBs are in operation with a network of 20,904 full -fledged \nbranches and more than 6300 Ultra Sma ll branches covering 644 notified districts in 27 \nStates and the Union Territory of Puducherry.  \n \nAll the RRBs  have adopted appropriate technology and migrated to core banking solutions \n(CBS) for better customer services. Further, as on 31 May, 2016, 50 out of 56 RRBs are \nhaving Point of Sale (PoS) certification. With increasing computerization, RRBs are now able \nto offer NEFT/RTGS, RuPay KCC, ATM Cards and other technology enabled Banking \nservices to their customers on the lines of Commercial Banks.  \n \nAll the Head Offices, Regional Offices and branches of RRBs are generally housed in pucca \npremises either own or r ented. Further, opening of about 36000 Customer Service Points \n(CSPs)/Business Correspondents (BCs) outlets by the RRBs has created rural outreach, \ninfrastructure and has resulted in better performance. \n \nSome of the RRBs have set up their own training infrastructure for internal training of staff \n \n(c) & (d):  There is no such proposal. \n \n(e): The Government of India based on the recommendations of Dr. K.C. Chakrabarty \nCommittee (Report of Committee on Recapitalisation of RRBs for improving CRAR) report, \nidentified 40 RRBs for recapitlisation assistance of Rs.2200 crore – (GoI Rs.1100 crore, \nSponsor Banks – Rs.770 crore and State Governments – Rs.330 crore).  An amount of \nRs.2208.62 crore has been released to 38 RRBs in 20 States as on 31st March, 2016. \n \nRRBs(Amendment) Act, 2015 notified on 12.05.2015, has inter-alia enhanced the authorized \ncapital of RRB from Rs.5 crore to Rs.2000 crore. \n \nAll the officers including the Chairpersons of RRBs  are being provided training on several \nkey functional areas like financial management, organizational development, IT and HR \nrelated aspects.  This has helped in knowledge, skill and attitude development of the staff of \nRRBs to meet the emerging challenges. \n \n___________ \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_498": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 498\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH NOVEMBER, 2016\nNATIONAL MEDICAL COMMISSION\n498. SHRI HUKUM SINGH:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether NITI Aayog has prepared a draft National Medical Commission Bill to set up a new\nbody replacing Medical Council of India (MCI);\n(b) if so, the details and objectives thereof; and\n(c) whether it is likely to be helpful to eradicate the large -scale corruption in granting the\napprovals for establishment of new medical colleges in the country?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) to (c) Yes, a draft National Medical Commission Bill is under consideration of NITI\nAayog. The proposed National Medical Commission shall have four boards, viz. Under -\nGraduate Medical Education Board (UGMEB), Post -Graduate Medical Education Board\n(PGMEB), Medical Assessment And Rating Board (MARB) and Board For Medical\nRegistration (BMR). UGMEB and PGMEB shall determine and prescribe standards and\noversee all aspects of medical education at undergraduate and postgraduate level\nrespectively. MARB shall deter mine the process of Assessment and Rating of Medical\nEducational Institutions as per the standards laid down by the UGMEB and PGMEB.\n******",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_499": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 499 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 18, 2016 \nKARTIKA 27, 1938 (Saka) \n \n \nInvestment Bottlenecks \n \n499. SHRI  KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state : \na) whether the projects of foreign investors/companies are delayed due to investment issues \nbottlenecks pertaining to taxation and environmental clearance discouraging the \npotential foreign investors; \n \nb) if so, the details thereof; and \n \nc) the steps taken by the Government to address the above issues and expedite the timely \nclearance of projects of foreign investors/companies and boost investor sentiments? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n \n(a) to (c) The projects of foreign investor/companies are not delayed due to investment \nbottlenecks pertaining to taxation.  \n \nThere are 39 categories of projects in the schedule which require Environmental \nClearance.  These proposals are processed as per the provisions under the Environmental Impact \nAssessment Notification, 2006.  These projects are accorded approval within the stipulated time \nframe after receiving documents from the Project proponent and State Government. \n \n****** "
  }
}